# Feature-Sliced Design in this project

> Generated by `nextjs-fsd init`. Edit freely — this file is the convention,
> not a lock file. Keep it in step with the code; a doc describing the
> previous shape is worse than no doc.

## Layout

```text
{{appDir}}/                        # Next.js App Router — routing only
├── layout.tsx               # composition root: fonts, <Providers>, global CSS
└── <route>/page.tsx         # re-exports an FSD page + its metadata, nothing else
{{srcDir}}/
├── _app/                    # FSD app layer — providers/, styles/, layouts/, api-routes/
├── _pages/<page>/           # one slice per route: ui/, index.ts (+ index.server.ts with a leaf)
├── features/<slice>/        # a whole user action, once two pages need it
├── entities/<slice>/        # a business object, once two features need it
└── shared/                  # infrastructure only — api/, auth/, config/, lib/, ui/
```

The FSD `app` and `pages` layers are named `_app` and `_pages` because
Next.js owns those names at the root.

**Add layers when a second consumer actually appears, not up front.**
Duplicating a component across two pages is cheaper to undo than an
`entities/` layer built for a reuse that never happens.

`widgets/` is left closed here, and that is **this project's** call rather than
the spec's. FSD v2.1 has it as an ordinary layer; its only caution is that a UI
block which is most of a page's content and is never reused should not be one.
The reasoning for closing it: a reusable block with its own fetching and state
is already a feature, so the widget/feature boundary would be one more decision
per screen buying nothing. Reach for `features/` first. Reopen it by deleting
this paragraph, not by asking the spec's permission.

When the second consumer does appear and what it shares is *data* — the types
and query hooks for one domain — `shared/<domain>/` is usually the honest
home: `shared/auth`, one file per capability. It bends "shared is
infrastructure" a little, and it buys not paying for a layer whose whole
promise is a third consumer that may never arrive. A `features/` slice is for
a user *action* two pages perform, which is a different thing. The first
project built this way passed seventeen pages without an `entities/` layer; do
not treat creating one as the default step.

**Two signals say that advice has run out.** Neither is a page count — a
`shared/` segment read by three pages can be perfectly correct.

The first is a **business rule landing in the segment**. Types and query hooks
are plumbing; a calculation, a format or a constraint that is *about* the
business is not, and `shared/` is defined as the layer with no business
meaning. When one shows up, the segment has become an entity and is only
filed under `shared/`.

The second is more expensive and quieter: **two `_pages` slices each writing
their own copy** of the same API resource. They cannot import each other, so
nothing makes the two agree — not review, not the compiler. Expect the type in
one to be missing fields the other has, both to sit on the same cache key with
one of them writing it as a bare string, and each to invalidate its own list
and not the other's. That is not the cheap duplication this document argues
for elsewhere; the cheap kind is one a compiler or a reader can see. Move it
below both, and let the question of which lower layer be settled by the
paragraph above.

## Import boundary

Imports point downwards only: `_app → _pages → widgets → features → entities
→ shared`. Two slices on the same layer never import each other, and a slice
is always imported through its `index.ts`:

```tsx
import { LoginForm } from "{{alias}}/_pages/login";             // ✅ public API
import { LoginForm } from "{{alias}}/_pages/login/ui/login-form"; // ❌ reaches inside
```

If two pages need the same thing, that is the signal it was never
page-scoped: move it down a layer instead of importing sideways. Two copies
in two pages while you are still unsure is the cheaper mistake.

## Two linters, on purpose

`{{lintCommand}}` runs both, and they are not redundant:

| | catches | when |
|---|---|---|
| **ESLint** (`eslint.fsd.mjs`) | this import points the wrong way, or reaches past a slice's `index.ts` | as you type, per file, in the editor |
| **steiger** (`steiger.config.ts`) | the same imports, plus a slice with no references, a layer sliced too finely, a segment named after its type, inconsistent naming | on demand, whole tree |

The overlap on imports is deliberate, not an oversight to clean up. steiger's
`fsd/forbidden-imports`, `fsd/no-cross-imports` and `fsd/no-public-api-sidestep`
do check the boundary — ESLint is not covering a gap in the plugin. It is
covering a gap in *timing*: steiger walks the tree when you run it, and a rule
you only meet in CI is a rule you have already built ten files on top of. Do
not delete the ESLint half on the grounds that steiger repeats it.

The other direction has no overlap at all: nothing in one file can show that a
slice has no consumers or that a layer has thirty slices, so that half only
works whole-tree.

`eslint.fsd.mjs` has no dependencies of its own: the boundary is expressed
with the core `no-restricted-imports` rule, and the layer order above is the
whole of it. One gotcha if you edit it — flat config **replaces** a rule's
options when a later block matches the same file instead of merging them, so
every pattern for a layer has to stay in that layer's single block. Split them
across two blocks and the earlier one silently stops applying.

When steiger says *"this slice has only one reference — consider merging
them"*, that is the "add layers when a second consumer appears" rule talking,
not a false positive. It is configured as a **warning** rather than an error,
because every slice has one consumer on the day it is created and failing the
build for that teaches people to delete the rule instead of the slice. Read it
anyway: a slice that stays at one consumer for good probably belongs inside
that consumer.

## `{{appDir}}/` stays thin

A route file re-exports an FSD page and nothing else:

```tsx
// {{appDir}}/login/page.tsx
export { LoginPage as default, metadata } from "{{alias}}/_pages/login/index.server";
```

Export `metadata` alongside the component — a route file that only
re-exports `default` silently drops the page's title.

The login page renders a client form, so its route imports from the
server-only entry: `index.server.ts` carries the page and its `metadata`,
while `index.ts` carries only the client-safe leaf. A server-only page has no
split — its route imports from the slice root. Either way a Client Component
importing the slice must never pull the server module into the client graph,
which Next.js rejects at build time.

## Segments

A slice gets only the segments it has code for. `ui/` alone is the common
case; add `model/` (state, hooks), `api/` (requests), `lib/` (helpers) or
`config/` (flags and settings) when that slice actually has them. An empty segment folder is noise.

Adding one later is the normal path, not a rewrite — re-run the same command
with the extra segment and it writes only what is missing, appending the new
exports to the slice's `index.ts` and leaving every existing file untouched:

```bash
nextjs-fsd generate slice features checkout --segments ui        # ui/ only
nextjs-fsd generate slice features checkout --segments ui,model  # adds model/
```

```text
{{srcDir}}/_pages/<page>/
├── ui/<page>-page.tsx  # server component + `metadata`
├── ui/<thing>.tsx      # "use client" only on the leaves that need it
├── index.ts            # client-safe public API (the leaf) — or the page, when server-only
└── index.server.ts     # with a leaf: the page + `metadata`, which is what {{appDir}}/ imports
```

## Server vs. client components

Default to a Server Component. `"use client"` goes on the leaf that needs
`useState`/`useEffect`/events — a page component stays a server component
while its form does not.

## Error copy, and i18n if you add one

Every failure a user reads is resolved from a catalog — `Record<code, string>`,
owned by the domain that raises those codes — and never from `error.message`,
which is a backend detail written for a log.

The generated sentences are a first draft in one language (`nextjs-fsd config
show` says which). That is all `locale` decides: there is no runtime i18n here,
and a project that adds one does not need a different shape from the CLI. A
catalog is a plain object, so build it from the translator where it is
rendered:

```tsx
const t = useTranslations("errors");
<FormError error={save.error} catalogs={[{ CONFLICT: t("conflict") }]} fallback={t("saveFailed")} />
```

The codes stay the keys. They are the API's contract, not copy, so they do not
translate.

{{#if (eq locale "th")}}
## Thai copy renders differently

Three things Latin-tuned defaults get wrong. All three are invisible until
someone reads the screen in Thai:

- **Never put negative tracking on Thai.** `tracking-tight` and friends
  collapse tone marks into the vowels beneath them, and Thai does not
  letterspace for emphasis either, so a wide `tracking-*` reads as broken
  rather than as a label.
- **Nothing Thai goes inside a `font-mono` element.** A mono face loaded with
  `subsets: ["latin"]` has no Thai glyph at all, so the string drops to a
  system fallback mid-word. Split the branch instead of wrapping both sides.
  Mono marks a value as literal — an id, a code, an account number — not
  prose, and not a column of numbers you only want lined up.
- **Line heights need room per text size.** Thai stacks up to two marks above
  a consonant plus a vowel below, so Tailwind's defaults (1.2 at `text-3xl`)
  drop one line's descenders into the next line's marks. Override
  `--text-*--line-height` for every size the theme actually uses.

{{/if}}

## Forms, and inline or toast

One question with one answer: does the user have to **fix** this, or only
**know** it?

- Something they must fix stays inline — every form error, and every failure
  that replaces a screen. `<FormError>` is that half. A toast is gone in
  seconds and points at no field, which is the wrong home for "your password
  is wrong" while someone is still typing.
- Something they only need to know goes to a toast — the result of a row
  button, a checkbox in a matrix, anything whose banner would otherwise sit
  far from the thing acted on.

A toast helper takes a **finished sentence**, never an error. Resolving a code
into copy belongs to the domain that raises it — the
`resolve<Domain>Error(error, fallback)` beside its catalog — so the toast layer
never learns that catalogs exist. Do not replace either half with a
QueryClient-wide `mutationCache.onError`: one handler cannot name the action
that failed, and it fires over the dialog errors that have to stay inline.

Forms get **one helper, not per-field state written again in every form**:
validate on submit, keep the messages in one place, and let the field
component own the `aria-invalid` / `aria-describedby` wiring. Client
validation is a courtesy — the API re-validates regardless — so name the
server-side rule each field mirrors in a comment, and a drift costs a round
trip rather than correctness.

## Generating

```bash
nextjs-fsd generate page <name>            # slice + {{appDir}}/<name>/page.tsx
nextjs-fsd generate slice features <name>  # a feature slice
nextjs-fsd generate layout admin           # _app/layouts + {{appDir}}/(admin)/layout.tsx
nextjs-fsd generate api-route health       # _app/api-routes + {{appDir}}/api/health/route.ts
nextjs-fsd add error-handling              # shared/api: ApiError, catalogs, client
nextjs-fsd add auth                        # shared/auth: session, guard, login page
```

Re-running a generate command extends what is there instead of refusing, so
`--errors` or `--client` on a page built bare earlier just adds the file. It
never rewrites one you already have — and it will not add a second route file
for a page that is already routed from somewhere else, because two `page.tsx`
resolving to the same URL is a Next.js build error.

Route handlers live in `_app/api-routes`, not `_pages`: a handler is not one
route's content, it is backend composition shared the way a layout is.
`{{appDir}}/api/<name>/route.ts` re-exports the logic as the HTTP method
(`export { getHealth as GET }`) and does nothing else — Next.js maps the URL
to the file, so the file stays the mapping and the work stays in the segment.

Some files stay at the project root because Next.js reads them there and
nowhere else: `middleware.js`, `instrumentation.js`. `init` never moves them,
and nothing generated imports them — they are routing concerns, not slices.
The legacy `pages/` router is out of scope for the same reason in reverse:
every generator here emits App Router shapes, so a `pages/` tree would be a
second routing layer no command knows how to extend. Database access goes in
`shared/db/` when it appears, next to nothing: queries and the caching around
them live in the same place, the way `shared/api` keeps the client next to
its QueryClient.

Layouts live in `_app/layouts`, not `_pages`: a layout is not one route's
content, it is what several routes have in common, and `{{appDir}}/(group)/layout.tsx`
re-exports it the same way a route file re-exports a page. `shadcn add <name>`
writes into `{{srcDir}}/shared/ui` — `components.json` is already aimed there.
